#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129918} :activity/id #uuid "35ebca4d-3a0a-5ee7-ba60-31eab0fe08b8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049078 :topic/id #uuid "d8d58724-adb9-5504-9910-b899e7c41cbf" :topic/math-academy-id 2046} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129918 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130335 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, a function might increase or decrease without bound when its input gets close to a specific value.\n\nFor example, consider the function whose graph is shown below:\n\n![](images/21/2163d96abc422e376581a693a6270da9d45ccc79d426d89f7ba9d1b046e2a254.png)\n\nAs the input $x$ gets closer to $1,$ the values of the function get larger and larger.\n\n![](images/c3/c367d69e8ef7369e81872f6f78460d8a2052a35e31d8f46e2b072d24a6d83f5a.png)\n\nWhen this happens, we say that the function approaches \"infinity\", or that it has an **infinite limit** at $x=1.$ We can write this mathematically, as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x) \\rightarrow \\infty\\quad \\text{as}\\quad x \\rightarrow 1\n$$\n\nIn words, this reads \"the function $f(x)$ approaches infinity as $x$ approaches $1$\".\n\nNow, consider the function $y = g(x),$ shown below. This is an example of a function that *decreases* without bound.\n\n![](images/a2/a21c625314d96dca5e724064bc35e67bed7c61fd56acc993cd7d281da29a2062.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ approaches negative infinity as $x$ approaches $1.$ So, we can write\n\n$$\ng(x) \\rightarrow -\\infty\\quad  \\text{as } \\quad x \\rightarrow 1.\n$$\n\nIn words, this reads \"the function $g(x)$ approaches negative infinity as $x$ approaches $1$\"." :tutorial/id #uuid "993e05cc-872c-5156-9300-df403918128f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2781 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "30bb5032-8b6d-589e-b245-21d8afdfc337" :step/math-academy-id 14196 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129927}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129919 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129098 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128914 :question/id #uuid "8ad1be49-273c-572d-8221-9605bfa16b7d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4012"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5dab76a-9d0c-5625-bba5-f903a55e3b7d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548052}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Infinite Behavior of a Function"} :step/id #uuid "e92dff94-995f-5d19-8b28-bee497c247ea" :step/math-academy-id 14204 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129926}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129924 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129103 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128919 :question/id #uuid "9d4d4b06-ace1-5d4a-828a-decea7c5b5fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-4053"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "408f96cb-5b2d-532b-874b-f9441dddfa30" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129736} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129741} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548043}] :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing One-Sided Infinite Behavior of a Function"} :step/id #uuid "add53e6f-c711-5389-b34a-63cf0fb453b0" :step/math-academy-id 14365 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129934}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129925 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129104 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128920 :question/id #uuid "5dee2a1e-7dd9-5c99-9f3b-5189360a2ef5" :question/math-academy-id "e-4054"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b81a25e-32d1-5f5f-9f8e-b18e5d354e2f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050763}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129737} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129856} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548045}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vertical Asymptotes"} :step/id #uuid "fa841aed-7d9e-52b6-a301-5ea7cab278ad" :step/math-academy-id 14366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129926 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130336 :tutorial/content "We know that some functions approach infinity as $x$ approaches a certain value, like the function $y=f(x)$ plotted below.\n\n![](images/da/da7878b503b9dff8f04c0f2c42e021353dc8b5fa744476fa0227bb954472c5ed.png)\n\nHere, the function shoots upwards when $x$ approaches $2$ *from the right*, that is, from the positive direction of the $x$-axis.\n\nWe say that the function approaches positive infinity as $x$ approaches $2$ from the right, and we write\n\n$$\nf(x) \\rightarrow \\infty \\quad \\text{as} \\quad x \\rightarrow 2^+,\n$$\n\nwhere the superscript $+$ next to the $2$ indicates that $x$ approaches $2$ from the right.\n\nSimilar to the case above, we can have a function that shoots upwards or downwards when $x$ approaches a finite value *from the left*, that is, from the negative direction of the $x$-axis. Consider the function $y=g(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/2c/2c3d79df60e6b0a5627f39e8397a1eb9dc6c5e48fb1e816f6adc5309545ffb29.png)\n\nWe say that the function approaches negative infinity as $x$ approaches $2$ from the left, and we write\n\n$$\ng(x) \\rightarrow -\\infty \\quad \\text{as} \\quad x \\rightarrow 2^-,\n$$\n\nwhere the superscript $-$ indicates that $x$ approaches $2$ from the left." :tutorial/id #uuid "8d16be94-aa13-52a0-bb7a-333c0a9d03ba" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2790 :tutorial/title "One-Sided Infinite Behavior of Functions"} :step/id #uuid "f39e32c5-50b4-5125-8c49-cf5bb0d9a1c7" :step/math-academy-id 14367 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129924}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129927 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129105 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128921 :question/id #uuid "b6212c1b-c1c8-5d0d-9984-1509b721e54d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4062"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d29ea673-5a84-5e48-b45d-85cade99566d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049573}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129729} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129731} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548061}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points Where a Function Approaches Infinity"} :step/id #uuid "2e1906c7-3dc3-5800-8af2-7c02ee80bf1b" :step/math-academy-id 14434 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129919}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129934 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130338 :tutorial/content "We know that some functions approach infinity as $x$ approaches a certain value from one side, like the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ plotted below.\n\n![](images/1b/1b1633611a2ea867e44845a8ba484e253671b9d5c74629f4a153ea79cf9a62e0.png)\n\nWe call the line $x=2$ a **vertical asymptote** of the functions $y = f(x)$ and $y=g(x).$\n\nAn asymptote is a line that a function gets very close to but never touches. In general, if a function $f(x)$ has an infinite limit (positive or negative) at a point $a,$ then we write\n\n$$\nf(x) \\rightarrow \\pm \\infty \\quad \\text{as} \\quad x \\rightarrow a^+\n$$\n\nor\n\n$$\nf(x)\\rightarrow \\pm\\infty  \\quad \\text{as} \\quad x \\rightarrow a^-\n$$\n\nand $x=a$ is a vertical asymptote of the function." :tutorial/id #uuid "8d3254a6-2a2f-5033-96a8-e1c1c6f2b8d8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2855 :tutorial/title "Vertical Asymptotes of Functions"} :step/id #uuid "c74a9040-1f67-519b-98bd-8fa831f5eddb" :step/math-academy-id 15203 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129925}}] :activity/title "Unbounded Behavior of Functions Near a Point" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129379}]}
